  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "What is the volume of a cube whose surface area is twice that of a cube with volume \\(1?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt{2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\sqrt{2}\\)",
    "D": "\\(4\\)",
    "E": "\\(8\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A cube of volume \\(1\\) has edge length \\(1\\), so its six square faces have total area \\(6\\).\n\nLet \\(s>0\\) be the edge length of the new cube. Its surface area is twice \\(6\\), so\n\\[\n6s^2=12,\\qquad s^2=2.\n\\]\nAn edge length is positive, so \\(s=\\sqrt2\\). Its volume is therefore\n\\[\ns^3=s^2\\cdot s=2\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(2\\sqrt2\\), choice C.",
